  "answer": "### Issue Presented\nIs leasing a residential unit at ₱15,000 per month subject to VAT?\n\n### Short Answer\nYes, leasing a residential unit at ₱15,000 per month is subject to VAT under certain conditions as outlined in the NIRC.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThe relevant provisions are found in the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC):\n- **Section 106**: Value-Added Tax on Sale of Goods or Properties\n- **Section 108**: Value-Added Tax on the Sale of Services, Including the Use or Lease of Properties\n\n### Legal Interpretation\nUnder **Section 106** of the NIRC, VAT is levied on the sale, barter, or exchange of goods or properties, which includes real properties held for lease in the ordinary course of trade or business. **Section 108** further specifies that VAT is also applicable to the lease of properties, indicating that the gross sales derived from leasing properties are subject to a 12% VAT. However, residential leases are generally exempt from VAT if the monthly rental does not exceed ₱15,000. Since the rental amount is exactly ₱15,000, it is at the threshold where VAT exemption applies.\n\n### Practical Application\nIn practice, if the lease of the residential unit is strictly for residential purposes and the monthly rent is ₱15,000 or less, it is exempt from VAT. However, if the lease is part of a business activity or if the lessor is VAT-registered and chooses to apply VAT, then the rental income would be subject to VAT. It is essential for lessors to determine their VAT registration status and the nature of the lease to ensure compliance with tax obligations.",
